  "strategy": "Approach and rationale\nThe original program gets stuck at a local minimum with a value of 0.380975. To break free from this, we will use a multi-resolution strategy with a structured initialization based on known constructions. This removes the parent's solution from the search space and uses a set of diverse candidate constructions to escape local optima. The approach combines periodic structures, frequency domain optimization, and a hybrid of gradient-based and evolutionary search. Specific goals are:\n1. Decrease the search space by anchoring to known constructions\n2. Use mathematical properties (sparsity, periodicity) to guide sampling\n3. Employ gradient-based optimization to refine promising configurations\n4. Ensure global exploration by sampling low-dimensional projections\nThis leverages mathematical knowledge that the optimal solution is sparse and periodic.\n\nMathematical specification\n- Input: Tokens generated by a stochastic process mapping disjoint interval overlaps to configurations\n- Representation: h is a vector with two nonzero values in a periodic structure, h(x) has compact support\n- Objective: $$\\max_k \\int h(x) (1-h(x+k)) dx$$\n- Constraints: $$0 \\le h(x) \\le 1, \\sum h(x) = \\frac{n}{2}$$\n- Formulation: We use a constrained optimization in product encoding, with soft-sparsity using L1 penalty on the Fourier transform coefficients\n\nImplementation procedure\n1. Initialization: \n   For each candidate:\n     a. Select one of: \n        EXTREME_SPARSE_PERIODIC1 = np.concatenate([[x]*p, [0]*(n-p)], -1) for p\u2248n/2\n        EXTREME_SPARSE_PERIODIC2 = Cartesian product in frequency domain\n     b. Generate candidate samples using known periodic patterns\n2. Optimization:\n   a. Use projected stochastic gradient with Polyak averaging and LineSearch\n   b. Key parameters: step_size=0.01, momentum=0.9, N_proj=5 for interpolation\n   c. For each candidate, run: \n        x0 = initial_candidate; \n        score = compute_c5(x0); \n        best = x0.copy()\n        for i in 200 iterations:\n            x_new = projected_gradient(x0, constraints)\n            self.feat_grid = compute_feature_grid(x_new)\n            if (score > c5_obj(x_new) and ... : accept candidate\n            score = min(score, c5_obj(x_new))\n            x0 = x_new\n\nSearch choices and useful variations \nDefine variations at multiple resolutions:\nSPARSE: two peaks of length 0.5 with separation\nSQUARES: alternate squares in each cell\nDENSE: multiple peaks\nAt each step, with 50% probability:\n- Apply structure preserving mutation\n- Add topK random 90% within viable parameters\n- Increase/decrease resolution by factor of 1.5\n\nCompute budget and robustness\nPrimary cost: The inner loop runs in O(n^2) but uses sparse in FFT. Total time is dominated by candidate evaluation (O(n log n)) versus optimization loop (O(p \u2248 10^3)). Monitor frequency of candidate evaluation and accept improvements; accept worst 10% worse solutions if gradient is improving.\n\nValidation and return contract\n- Check h(x) in [0,1], sum(h)=n/2 for all candidates\n- Re-evaluate final candidate through compute_c5\n- Maintain history of best solution with improvement counts\n- Handle missing transforms and projection failures by clipping and sum normalization\nAll candidates are generated in 128/96/64 vector space, projections with 16-bit quantization",
